# Finite Sketch geometry production review

The production/stage and original helper identities, exact baseline bytes and current observations are preserved under baseline. The focused 28 tests ran directly, with their detailed geometry observations redirected to this work folder. Source and published result bytes matched before and after; no app/results or implementation source was changed. Additional checks compare 528 zero-tolerance hit points to an independently implemented polygon ray-crossing method, cover signed/zero line roundtrips, retain opaque rotated own-prototype metadata without SVG rendering, and reject excessive native JSON/non-finite values. The current core/client match exact staged bytes.

The 17 comparative contexts and 35 direct helper calls describe only the geometry campaign: eight diamond, eight size, 18 restore and one serializer call. The separate legacy six describes its six-element restoration corpus; it has additional edited/dot restoration, serialization and predicate calls, and the focused collector calls that legacy function again after the test subprocess. Counts must not be presented as a whole-suite invocation total. Full original UI/renderer/binding/default repair is not tested. Tiny/degenerate diamond anchor clamping and transparent-interior geometric selection are explicit deviations.

All 90 retained snapshots, 51 staged source identities, 11 baseline records, authenticated original archive/bundle/source map, 25 exact helper byte ranges and MIT notice are accounted for. source-reference-validation.json separately verifies the actual five-entry references inventory, including released map contents and explicit externally fetched pinned type-only source distinction. The main review originally inspected an absent sources collection; its sourceReferences is therefore empty, and no behavior was rerun to repair that bookkeeping.

Normal tests write geometry details only to an owned observation path or ignored app .local, never published results. Their persistence fixture leaves owned .local/test-fixtures folders. The collector publishes results only after its finite passing run but currently has no subprocess timeout or raw-log cap; runaway-output robustness is not established. No full product parity, original-grade security, timing/memory comparison or universal superiority conclusion is made.
